.nh
.TH "BURROW" "1" "Oct 2026" "burrow 0.1.0-dev" "burrow manual"

.SH NAME
burrow-analyze - read-only disk explorer


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBburrow analyze [path...] [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Explore where disk space went.

.PP
Read-only: navigate the tree with the arrow keys. The only way to delete
things in burrow is the clean flow's review screen.

.PP
The --json schema is stable:
  {"path":"...","total_size":N,"total_files":N,
   "entries":[...],"largest":[...],
   "scan_status":"complete|partial|unavailable","scan_status_reason":"..."}

.PP
When stdout is not a terminal (or --json is set), analyze behaves like
--json and never waits for input. --json accepts a single path.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-a\fP, \fB--apparent\fP[=false]
	show apparent size instead of on-disk usage

.PP
\fB--cross-mounts\fP[=false]
	descend into nested mounts on other filesystems

.PP
\fB--depth\fP=0
	non-interactive depth (with --json); 0 = unlimited

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for analyze

.PP
\fB-H\fP, \fB--no-hidden\fP[=false]
	skip dotfiles

.PP
\fB-x\fP, \fB--one-file-system\fP[=false]
	do not descend across mount points (default comes from config; an explicit flag wins)

.PP
\fB--scan-timeout\fP=1m30s
	per-root scan deadline

.PP
\fB-t\fP, \fB--top\fP=10
	non-interactive top-N largest entries (with --json)


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--color\fP="auto"
	terminal color policy: auto|always|never

.PP
\fB--config\fP=""
	override the config file path

.PP
\fB--dry-run\fP[=false]
	never mutate; print the plan and exit

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	machine-readable output

.PP
\fB--no-color\fP[=false]
	disable color (same as --color=never)

.PP
\fB--plain\fP[=false]
	non-interactive plain-text output instead of the TUI

.PP
\fB-y\fP, \fB--yes\fP[=false]
	skip the interactive confirm


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBburrow(1)\fP
